   362	
   363	## The nullity-16 Rees fibers: eight complete, ten block at the modular fiber
   364	
   365	[`nullity16_rees_fibers.py`](nullity16_rees_fibers.py) applies the one-pass normal-form reduction to the 126
   366	four-Pfaffian coordinates of the eight rows that timed out above, then computes the Nakayama-reduced Rees special
   367	fiber over `Q(i)` and modulo the split prime 32009 under both square roots of -1 (10754 and 21255). The retained
   368	[`nullity16-rees-fibers-result.json`](nullity16-rees-fibers-result.json) certifies these survivor selections:
   369	
   370	| Incidence family | Independent survivors | Coefficient monomials |
   371	| --- | ---: | ---: |
   372	| `E8bar->N+`, `E8bar->N-` | 103 of 126 | 3,741 |
   373	| `W8->N+`, `W8->N-` | 124 of 126 | 2,589 |
   374	| `X+->N+`, `X-->N-` | 69 of 126 | 727 |
   375	| `X+->N-`, `X-->N+` | 69 of 126 | 731 |
   376	
   377	Eight incidences complete, with the rational fiber and both modular fibers agreeing in component count, degree and
   378	projective dimension:
   379	
   380	| Incidence | Embedding rank | Special fiber |
   381	| --- | ---: | --- |
   382	| `B9->N+`, `B9->N-` | 3 | the plane `P^2` |
   383	| `V->N+`, `V->N-` | 3 | the conic `y2^2 - y1*y3` in `P^2` |
   384	| `VB->N+`, `VB->N-`, `Va+->N+`, `Va-->N-` | 1 | one point |
   385	
   386	The generator-deletion mutation on `B9->N+` drops the first Nakayama generator (original coordinate 23) and changes
   387	the fiber from degree 2 to degree 1, so the check detects a missing generator. The ten incidences from the nullity-8
   388	sources `E8bar`, `Iso`, `W8`, `X+` and `X-` complete the Nakayama count and embedding and then reach the 300-second
   389	timeout at `modular_rees_fiber`; the self-test admits exactly that stage for them. Every Singular call runs in a
   390	cgroup scope with a 16 GiB `MemoryMax` and no swap, one at a time; a 4 GiB cap ends the four `E8bar` and `W8` fibers in
   391	Singular's `no more memory` allocation failure at 2.5 GB resident instead of the timeout.
   392	
   393	The record has `pass=false` because ten rows block. It certifies the survivor ranks, the eight fibers above, their
   394	characteristic agreement and the detected mutation. It certifies no fiber for the ten blocked rows, no real fiber
   395	and no attaching map. A completed modular fiber for a blocked row, a characteristic disagreement, a standard basis
   396	point inside an embedding span, or a surviving mutation falsifies it.
   397	
   398	## Investigated questions
   399	
   400	### Does the antiholomorphic first-order part survive on a boundary kernel?
   401	
   402	The question matters because a surviving antiholomorphic restriction would make the first-order Gauss map only
   403	real-linear and would rule out a complex-algebraic description at that order. The cheapest decisive probe splits the
   404	bordered-matrix derivative and restricts its antiholomorphic part to exact kernel frames at canonical and moved
   405	points. The restriction vanishes at all 21 points, for the scalar-row mechanism above. A nonzero restriction at any
   406	exact point falsifies the result. Evidence: [`incidence-census-result.json`](incidence-census-result.json).
   407	
   408	### Is the zero scalar row sampled or structural?
   409	
   410	The question matters because a sampled vanishing result gives no control between tested representatives. The cheapest
   411	decisive probe is the symbolic projection of the bordered-kernel equation above. **Inferred:** every partner of every
   412	nonzero doubly pure element has zero scalar coordinate, so the antiholomorphic restriction vanishes on the whole
   413	nonzero locus. A valid partner with nonzero `e_0` or `e_16` coordinate, or a failed identity in the retained proof
   414	record, falsifies the result. Evidence: [`partner-purity-result.json`](partner-purity-result.json).
